<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>
                                               February 26, 1982



<i>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</i>

     Operating earnings of $39.7 million in 1981 amounted to=20
15.2% of beginning equity capital (valuing securities at cost)=20
compared to 17.8% in 1980.  Our new plan that allows stockholders=20
to designate corporate charitable contributions (detailed later)=20
reduced earnings by about $900,000 in 1981.  This program, which=20
we expect to continue subject to annual evaluation of our=20
corporate tax position, had not been initiated in 1980.


<i>Non-Controlled Ownership Earnings</i>

     In the 1980 annual report we discussed extensively the=20
concept of non-controlled ownership earnings, i.e., Berkshire=92s=20
share of the undistributed earnings of companies we don=92t control=20
or significantly influence but in which we, nevertheless, have=20
important investments. (We will be glad to make available to new=20
or prospective shareholders copies of that discussion or others=20
from earlier reports to which we refer in this report.) No=20
portion of those undistributed earnings is included in the=20
operating earnings of Berkshire.

     However, our belief is that, in aggregate, those=20
undistributed and, therefore, unrecorded earnings will be=20
translated into tangible value for Berkshire shareholders just as=20
surely as if subsidiaries we control had earned, retained - and=20
reported - similar earnings.

     We know that this translation of non-controlled ownership=20
earnings into corresponding realized and unrealized capital gains=20
for Berkshire will be extremely irregular as to time of=20
occurrence.  While market values track business values quite well=20
over long periods, in any given year the relationship can gyrate=20
capriciously.  Market recognition of retained earnings also will=20
be unevenly realized among companies.  It will be disappointingly=20
low or negative in cases where earnings are employed non-
productively, and far greater than dollar-for-dollar of retained=20
earnings in cases of companies that achieve high returns with=20
their augmented capital.  Overall, if a group of non-controlled=20
companies is selected with reasonable skill, the group result=20
should be quite satisfactory.

     In aggregate, our non-controlled business interests have=20
more favorable underlying economic characteristics than our=20
controlled businesses.  That=92s understandable; the area of choice=20
has been far wider.  Small portions of exceptionally good=20
businesses are usually available in the securities markets at=20
reasonable prices.  But such businesses are available for=20
purchase in their entirety only rarely, and then almost always at=20
high prices.


<i>General Acquisition Behavior</i>

     As our history indicates, we are comfortable both with total=20
ownership of businesses and with marketable securities=20
representing small portions of businesses.  We continually look=20
for ways to employ large sums in each area. (But we try to avoid=20
small commitments - =93If something=92s not worth doing at all, it=92s=20
not worth doing well=94.) Indeed, the liquidity requirements of our=20
insurance and trading stamp businesses mandate major investments=20
in marketable securities.

     Our acquisition decisions will be aimed at maximizing real=20
economic benefits, not at maximizing either managerial domain or=20
reported numbers for accounting purposes. (In the long run,=20
managements stressing accounting appearance over economic=20
substance usually achieve little of either.)

     Regardless of the impact upon immediately reportable=20
earnings, we would rather buy 10% of Wonderful Business T at X=20
per share than 100% of T at 2X per share.  Most corporate=20
managers prefer just the reverse, and have no shortage of stated=20
rationales for their behavior.

     However, we suspect three motivations - usually unspoken -=20
to be, singly or in combination, the important ones in most high-
premium takeovers:

     (1) Leaders, business or otherwise, seldom are deficient in=20
         animal spirits and often relish increased activity and=20
         challenge.  At Berkshire, the corporate pulse never=20
         beats faster than when an acquisition is in prospect.

     (2) Most organizations, business or otherwise, measure=20
         themselves, are measured by others, and compensate their=20
         managers far more by the yardstick of size than by any=20
         other yardstick. (Ask a <i>Fortune 500</i> manager where his=20
         corporation stands on that famous list and, invariably,=20
         the number responded will be from the list ranked by=20
         size of sales; he may well not even know where his=20
         corporation places on the list <i>Fortune</i> just as=20
         faithfully compiles ranking the same 500 corporations by=20
         profitability.)

     (3) Many managements apparently were overexposed in=20
         impressionable childhood years to the story in which the=20
         imprisoned handsome prince is released from a toad=92s=20
         body by a kiss from a beautiful princess.  Consequently,=20
         they are certain their managerial kiss will do wonders=20
         for the profitability of Company T(arget).

            Such optimism is essential.  Absent that rosy view,=20
         why else should the shareholders of Company A(cquisitor)=20
         want to own an interest in T at the 2X takeover cost=20
         rather than at the X market price they would pay if they=20
         made direct purchases on their own?

            In other words, investors can always buy toads at the=20
         going price for toads.  If investors instead bankroll=20
         princesses who wish to pay double for the right to kiss=20
         the toad, those kisses had better pack some real=20
         dynamite.  We=92ve observed many kisses but very few=20
         miracles.  Nevertheless, many managerial princesses=20
         remain serenely confident about the future potency of=20
         their kisses - even after their corporate backyards are=20
         knee-deep in unresponsive toads.

     In fairness, we should acknowledge that some acquisition=20
records have been dazzling.  Two major categories stand out.

     The first involves companies that, through design or=20
accident, have purchased only businesses that are particularly=20
well adapted to an inflationary environment.  Such favored=20
business must have two characteristics: (1) an ability to=20
increase prices rather easily (even when product demand is flat=20
and capacity is not fully utilized) without fear of significant=20
loss of either market share or unit volume, and (2) an ability to=20
accommodate large dollar volume increases in business (often=20
produced more by inflation than by real growth) with only minor=20
additional investment of capital.  Managers of ordinary ability,=20
focusing solely on acquisition possibilities meeting these tests,=20
have achieved excellent results in recent decades.  However, very=20
few enterprises possess both characteristics, and competition to=20
buy those that do has now become fierce to the point of being=20
self-defeating.

     The second category involves the managerial superstars - men=20
who can recognize that rare prince who is disguised as a toad,=20
and who have managerial abilities that enable them to peel away=20
the disguise.  We salute such managers as Ben Heineman at=20
Northwest Industries, Henry Singleton at Teledyne, Erwin Zaban at=20
National Service Industries, and especially Tom Murphy at Capital=20
Cities Communications (a real managerial =93twofer=94, whose=20
acquisition efforts have been properly focused in Category 1 and=20
whose operating talents also make him a leader of Category 2). =20
From both direct and vicarious experience, we recognize the=20
difficulty and rarity of these executives=92 achievements. (So do=20
they; these champs have made very few deals in recent years, and=20
often have found repurchase of their own shares to be the most=20
sensible employment of corporate capital.)

     Your Chairman, unfortunately, does not qualify for Category=20
2.  And, despite a reasonably good understanding of the economic=20
factors compelling concentration in Category 1, our actual=20
acquisition activity in that category has been sporadic and=20
inadequate.  Our preaching was better than our performance. (We=20
neglected the Noah principle: predicting rain doesn=92t count,=20
building arks does.)

     We have tried occasionally to buy toads at bargain prices=20
with results that have been chronicled in past reports.  Clearly=20
our kisses fell flat.  We have done well with a couple of princes=20
- but they were princes when purchased.  At least our kisses=20
didn=92t turn them into toads.  And, finally, we have occasionally=20
been quite successful in purchasing fractional interests in=20
easily-identifiable princes at toad-like prices.


<i>Berkshire Acquisition Objectives</i>

     We will continue to seek the acquisition of businesses in=20
their entirety at prices that will make sense, even should the=20
future of the acquired enterprise develop much along the lines of=20
its past.  We may very well pay a fairly fancy price for a=20
Category 1 business if we are reasonably confident of what we are=20
getting.  But we will not normally pay a lot in any purchase for=20
what we are supposed to bring to the party - for we find that we=20
ordinarily don=92t bring a lot.

     During 1981 we came quite close to a major purchase=20
involving both a business and a manager we liked very much. =20
However, the price finally demanded, considering alternative uses=20
for the funds involved, would have left our owners worse off than=20
before the purchase.  The empire would have been larger, but the=20
citizenry would have been poorer.

     Although we had no success in 1981, from time to time in the=20
future we will be able to purchase 100% of businesses meeting our=20
standards.  Additionally, we expect an occasional offering of a=20
major =93non-voting partnership=94 as discussed under the Pinkerton=92s=20
heading on page 47 of this report.  We welcome suggestions=20
regarding such companies where we, as a substantial junior=20
partner, can achieve good economic results while furthering the=20
long-term objectives of present owners and managers.

     Currently, we find values most easily obtained through the=20
open-market purchase of fractional positions in companies with=20
excellent business franchises and competent, honest managements. =20
We never expect to run these companies, but we do expect to=20
profit from them.

     We expect that undistributed earnings from such companies=20
will produce full value (subject to tax when realized) for=20
Berkshire and its shareholders.  If they don=92t, we have made=20
mistakes as to either: (1) the management we have elected to=20
join; (2) the future economics of the business; or (3) the price=20
we have paid.

     We have made plenty of such mistakes - both in the purchase=20
of non-controlling and controlling interests in businesses. =20
Category (2) miscalculations are the most common.  Of course, it=20
is necessary to dig deep into our history to find illustrations=20
of such mistakes - sometimes as deep as two or three months back. =20
For example, last year your Chairman volunteered his expert=20
opinion on the rosy future of the aluminum business.  Several=20
minor adjustments to that opinion - now aggregating approximately=20
180 degrees - have since been required.

     For personal as well as more objective reasons, however, we=20
generally have been able to correct such mistakes far more=20
quickly in the case of non-controlled businesses (marketable=20
securities) than in the case of controlled subsidiaries.  Lack of=20
control, in effect, often has turned out to be an economic plus.

     As we mentioned last year, the magnitude of our non-recorded=20
=93ownership=94 earnings has grown to the point where their total is=20
greater than our reported operating earnings.  We expect this=20
situation will continue.  In just four ownership positions in=20
this category - GEICO Corporation, General Foods Corporation, R.=20
J. Reynolds Industries, Inc. and The Washington Post Company -=20
our share of undistributed and therefore unrecorded earnings=20
probably will total well over $35 million in 1982.  The=20
accounting rules that entirely ignore these undistributed=20
earnings diminish the utility of our annual return on equity=20
calculation, or any other single year measure of economic=20
performance.


<i>Long-Term Corporate Performance</i>

     In measuring long-term economic performance, equities held=20
by our insurance subsidiaries are valued at market subject to a=20
charge reflecting the amount of taxes that would have to be paid=20
if unrealized gains were actually realized.  If we are correct in=20
the premise stressed in the preceding section of this report, our=20
unreported ownership earnings will find their way, irregularly=20
but inevitably, into our net worth.  To date, this has been the=20
case.

     An even purer calculation of performance would involve a=20
valuation of bonds and non-insurance held equities at market. =20
However, GAAP accounting does not prescribe this procedure, and=20
the added purity would change results only very slightly.  Should=20
any valuation difference widen to significant proportions, as it=20
has at most major insurance companies, we will report its effect=20
to you.

     On a GAAP basis, during the present management=92s term of=20
seventeen years, book value has increased from $19.46 per share=20
to $526.02 per share, or 21.1% compounded annually.  This rate of=20
return number is highly likely to drift downward in future years. =20
We hope, however, that it can be maintained significantly above=20
the rate of return achieved by the average large American=20
corporation.

     Over half of the large gain in Berkshire=92s net worth during=20
1981 - it totaled $124 million, or about 31% - resulted from the=20
market performance of a single investment, GEICO Corporation.  In=20
aggregate, our market gain from securities during the year=20
considerably outstripped the gain in underlying business values. =20
Such market variations will not always be on the pleasant side.

     In past reports we have explained how inflation has caused=20
our apparently satisfactory long-term corporate performance to be=20
illusory as a measure of true investment results for our owners. =20
We applaud the efforts of Federal Reserve Chairman Volcker and=20
note the currently more moderate increases in various price=20
indices.  Nevertheless, our views regarding long-term=20
inflationary trends are as negative as ever.  Like virginity, a=20
stable price level seems capable of maintenance, but not of=20
restoration.

     Despite the overriding importance of inflation in the=20
investment equation, we will not punish you further with another=20
full recital of our views; inflation itself will be punishment=20
enough. (Copies of previous discussions are available for=20
masochists.) But, because of the unrelenting destruction of=20
currency values, our corporate efforts will continue to do a much=20
better job of filling your wallet than of filling your stomach.


<i>Equity Value-Added</i>

     An additional factor should further subdue any residual=20
enthusiasm you may retain regarding our long-term rate of return. =20
The economic case justifying equity investment is that, in=20
aggregate, additional earnings above passive investment returns -=20
interest on fixed-income securities - will be derived through the=20
employment of managerial and entrepreneurial skills in=20
conjunction with that equity capital.  Furthermore, the case says=20
that since the equity capital position is associated with greater=20
risk than passive forms of investment, it is =93entitled=94 to higher=20
returns.  A =93value-added=94 bonus from equity capital seems natural=20
and certain.

     But is it?  Several decades back, a return on equity of as=20
little as 10% enabled a corporation to be classified as a =93good=94=20
business - i.e., one in which a dollar reinvested in the business=20
logically could be expected to be valued by the market at more=20
than one hundred cents.  For, with long-term taxable bonds=20
yielding 5% and long-term tax-exempt bonds 3%, a business=20
operation that could utilize equity capital at 10% clearly was=20
worth some premium to investors over the equity capital employed. =20
That was true even though a combination of taxes on dividends and=20
on capital gains would reduce the 10% earned by the corporation=20
to perhaps 6%-8% in the hands of the individual investor.

     Investment markets recognized this truth.  During that=20
earlier period, American business earned an average of 11% or so=20
on equity capital employed and stocks, in aggregate, sold at=20
valuations far above that equity capital (book value), averaging=20
over 150 cents on the dollar.  Most businesses were =93good=94=20
businesses because they earned far more than their keep (the=20
return on long-term passive money).  The value-added produced by=20
equity investment, in aggregate, was substantial.

     That day is gone.  But the lessons learned during its=20
existence are difficult to discard.  While investors and managers=20
must place their feet in the future, their memories and nervous=20
systems often remain plugged into the past.  It is much easier=20
for investors to utilize historic p/e ratios or for managers to=20
utilize historic business valuation yardsticks than it is for=20
either group to rethink their premises daily.  When change is=20
slow, constant rethinking is actually undesirable; it achieves=20
little and slows response time.  But when change is great,=20
yesterday=92s assumptions can be retained only at great cost.  And=20
the pace of economic change has become breathtaking.

     During the past year, long-term taxable bond yields exceeded=20
16% and long-term tax-exempts 14%.  The total return achieved=20
from such tax-exempts, of course, goes directly into the pocket=20
of the individual owner.  Meanwhile, American business is=20
producing earnings of only about 14% on equity.  And this 14%=20
will be substantially reduced by taxation before it can be banked=20
by the individual owner.  The extent of such shrinkage depends=20
upon the dividend policy of the corporation and the tax rates=20
applicable to the investor.

     Thus, <i>with interest rates on passive investments at late=20
1981 levels,</i> a typical American business is no longer worth one=20
hundred cents on the dollar to owners who are individuals. (If=20
the business is owned by pension funds or other tax-exempt=20
investors, the arithmetic, although still unenticing, changes=20
substantially for the better.) Assume an investor in a 50% tax=20
bracket; if our typical company pays out all earnings, the income=20
return to the investor will be equivalent to that from a 7% tax-
exempt bond.  And, if conditions persist - if all earnings are=20
paid out and return on equity stays at 14% - the 7% tax-exempt=20
equivalent to the higher-bracket individual investor is just as=20
frozen as is the coupon on a tax-exempt bond.  Such a perpetual=20
7% tax-exempt bond might be worth fifty cents on the dollar as=20
this is written.

     If, on the other hand, all earnings of our typical American=20
business are retained and return on equity again remains=20
constant, earnings will grow at 14% per year.  If the p/e ratio=20
remains constant, the price of our typical stock will also grow=20
at 14% per year.  But that 14% is not yet in the pocket of the=20
shareholder.  Putting it there will require the payment of a=20
capital gains tax, presently assessed at a maximum rate of 20%. =20
This net return, of course, works out to a poorer rate of return=20
than the currently available passive after-tax rate.

     Unless passive rates fall, companies achieving 14% per year=20
gains in earnings per share while paying no cash dividend are an=20
economic failure for their individual shareholders.  The returns=20
from passive capital outstrip the returns from active capital. =20
This is an unpleasant fact for both investors and corporate=20
managers and, therefore, one they may wish to ignore.  But facts=20
do not cease to exist, either because they are unpleasant or=20
because they are ignored.

     Most American businesses pay out a significant portion of=20
their earnings and thus fall between the two examples.  And most=20
American businesses are currently =93bad=94 businesses economically -=20
producing less for their individual investors after-tax than the=20
tax-exempt passive rate of return on money.  Of course, some=20
high-return businesses still remain attractive, even under=20
present conditions.  But American equity capital, in aggregate,=20
produces no value-added for individual investors.

     It should be stressed that this depressing situation does=20
not occur because corporations are jumping, economically, less=20
high than previously.  In fact, they are jumping somewhat higher:=20
return on equity has improved a few points in the past decade. =20
But the crossbar of passive return has been elevated much faster. =20
Unhappily, most companies can do little but hope that the bar=20
will be lowered significantly; there are few industries in which=20
the prospects seem bright for substantial gains in return on=20
equity.

     Inflationary experience and expectations will be major (but=20
not the only) factors affecting the height of the crossbar in=20
future years.  If the causes of long-term inflation can be=20
tempered, passive returns are likely to fall and the intrinsic=20
position of American equity capital should significantly improve. =20
Many businesses that now must be classified as economically =93bad=94=20
would be restored to the =93good=94 category under such=20
circumstances.

     A further, particularly ironic, punishment is inflicted by=20
an inflationary environment upon the owners of the =93bad=94=20
business.  To continue operating in its present mode, such a low-
return business usually must retain much of its earnings - no=20
matter what penalty such a policy produces for shareholders.

     Reason, of course, would prescribe just the opposite policy. =20
An individual, stuck with a 5% bond with many years to run before=20
maturity, does not take the coupons from that bond and pay one=20
hundred cents on the dollar for more 5% bonds while similar bonds=20
are available at, say, forty cents on the dollar.  Instead, he=20
takes those coupons from his low-return bond and - if inclined to=20
reinvest - looks for the highest return with safety currently=20
available.  Good money is not thrown after bad.

     What makes sense for the bondholder makes sense for the=20
shareholder.  Logically, a company with historic <i>and prospective</i>=20
high returns on equity should retain much or all of its earnings=20
so that shareholders can earn premium returns on enhanced=20
capital.  Conversely, low returns on corporate equity would=20
suggest a very high dividend payout so that owners could direct=20
capital toward more attractive areas. (The Scriptures concur.  In=20
the parable of the talents, the two high-earning servants are=20
rewarded with 100% retention of earnings and encouraged to expand=20
their operations.  However, the non-earning third servant is not=20
only chastised - =93wicked and slothful=94 - but also is required to=20
redirect all of his capital to the top performer.  <i>Matthew 25:=20
14-30)</i>

     But inflation takes us through the looking glass into the=20
upside-down world of <i>Alice in Wonderland.</i>  When prices=20
continuously rise, the =93bad=94 business must retain every nickel=20
that it can.  Not because it is attractive as a repository for=20
equity capital, but precisely because it is so unattractive, the=20
low-return business must follow a high retention policy.  If it=20
wishes to continue operating in the future as it has in the past=20
- and most entities, including businesses, do - it simply has no=20
choice.

     For inflation acts as a gigantic corporate tapeworm.  That=20
tapeworm preemptively consumes its requisite daily diet of=20
investment dollars regardless of the health of the host organism. =20
Whatever the level of reported profits (even if nil), more=20
dollars for receivables, inventory and fixed assets are=20
continuously required by the business in order to merely match=20
the unit volume of the previous year.  The less prosperous the=20
enterprise, the greater the proportion of available sustenance=20
claimed by the tapeworm.

     Under present conditions, a business earning 8% or 10% on=20
equity often has no leftovers for expansion, debt reduction or=20
=93real=94 dividends.  The tapeworm of inflation simply cleans the=20
plate. (The low-return company=92s inability to pay dividends,=20
understandably, is often disguised.  Corporate America=20
increasingly is turning to dividend reinvestment plans, sometimes=20
even embodying a discount arrangement that all but forces=20
shareholders to reinvest.  Other companies sell newly issued=20
shares to Peter in order to pay dividends to Paul.  Beware of=20
=93dividends=94 that can be paid out only if someone promises to=20
replace the capital distributed.)

     Berkshire continues to retain its earnings for offensive,=20
not defensive or obligatory, reasons.  But in no way are we=20
immune from the pressures that escalating passive returns exert=20
on equity capital.  We continue to clear the crossbar of after-
tax passive return - but barely.  Our historic 21% return - not=20
at all assured for the future - still provides, after the current=20
capital gain tax rate (which we expect to rise considerably in=20
future years), a modest margin over current after-tax rates on=20
passive money.  It would be a bit humiliating to have our=20
corporate value-added turn negative.  But it can happen here as=20
it has elsewhere, either from events outside anyone=92s control or=20
from poor relative adaptation on our part.


