<i>Insurance Industry Conditions</i>

     =93Forecasts=94, said Sam Goldwyn, =93are dangerous, particularly=20
those about the future.=94 (Berkshire shareholders may have reached=20
a similar conclusion after rereading our past annual reports=20
featuring your Chairman=92s prescient analysis of textile=20
prospects.)

     There is no danger, however, in forecasting that 1982 will=20
be the worst year in recent history for insurance underwriting. =20
That result already has been guaranteed by present pricing=20
behavior, coupled with the term nature of the insurance contract.

     While many auto policies are priced and sold at six-month=20
intervals - and many property policies are sold for a three-year=20
term - a weighted average of the duration of all property-
casualty insurance policies probably runs a little under twelve=20
months.  And prices for the insurance coverage, of course, are=20
frozen for the life of the contract.  Thus, this year=92s sales=20
contracts (=93premium written=94 in the parlance of the industry)=20
determine about one-half of next year=92s level of revenue=20
(=93premiums earned=94).  The remaining half will be determined by=20
sales contracts written next year that will be about 50% earned=20
in that year.  The profitability consequences are automatic: if=20
you make a mistake in pricing, you have to live with it for an=20
uncomfortable period of time.

     Note in the table below the year-over-year gain in industry-
wide premiums written and the impact that it has on the current=20
and following year=92s level of underwriting profitability.  The=20
result is exactly as you would expect in an inflationary world. =20
When the volume gain is well up in double digits, it bodes well=20
for profitability trends in the current and following year.  When=20
the industry volume gain is small, underwriting experience very=20
shortly will get worse, no matter how unsatisfactory the current=20
level.

