     As Pogo would say, =93The future isn=92t what it used to be.=94=20
Current pricing practices promise devastating results,=20
particularly if the respite from major natural disasters that the=20
industry has enjoyed in recent years should end.  <i>For=20
underwriting experience has been getting worse in spite of good=20
luck, not because of bad luck.</i>  In recent years hurricanes have=20
stayed at sea and motorists have reduced their driving.  They=20
won=92t always be so obliging.

     And, of course the twin inflations, monetary and =93social=94=20
(the tendency of courts and juries to stretch the coverage of=20
policies beyond what insurers, relying upon contract terminology=20
and precedent, had expected), are unstoppable.  Costs of=20
repairing both property and people - and the extent to which=20
these repairs are deemed to be the responsibility of the insurer=20
- will advance relentlessly.

     Absent any bad luck (catastrophes, increased driving, etc.),=20
an immediate industry volume gain of at least 10% per year=20
probably is necessary to <i>stabilize</i> the record level of=20
underwriting losses that will automatically prevail in mid-1982. =20
(Most underwriters expect incurred losses in aggregate to rise at=20
least 10% annually; each, of course, counts on getting less than=20
his share.) Every percentage point of annual premium growth below=20
the 10% equilibrium figure quickens the pace of deterioration. =20
Quarterly data in 1981 underscore the conclusion that a terrible=20
underwriting picture is worsening at an accelerating rate.

     In the 1980 annual report we discussed the investment=20
policies that have destroyed the integrity of many insurers=92=20
balance sheets, forcing them to abandon underwriting discipline=20
and write business at any price in order to avoid negative cash=20
flow.  It was clear that insurers with large holdings of bonds=20
valued, for accounting purposes, at nonsensically high prices=20
would have little choice but to keep the money revolving by=20
selling large numbers of policies at nonsensically low prices. =20
Such insurers necessarily fear a major decrease in volume more=20
than they fear a major underwriting loss.

     But, unfortunately, all insurers are affected; it=92s=20
difficult to price much differently than your most threatened=20
competitor.  This pressure continues unabated and adds a new=20
motivation to the others that drive many insurance managers to=20
push for business; worship of size over profitability, and the=20
fear that market share surrendered never can be regained.

     Whatever the reasons, we believe it is true that virtually=20
no major property-casualty insurer - despite protests by the=20
entire industry that rates are inadequate and great selectivity=20
should be exercised - has been willing to turn down business to=20
the point where cash flow has turned significantly negative. =20
Absent such a willingness, prices will remain under severe=20
pressure.

     Commentators continue to talk of the underwriting cycle,=20
usually implying a regularity of rhythm and a relatively constant=20
midpoint of profitability Our own view is different.  We believe=20
that very large, although obviously varying, underwriting losses=20
will be the norm for the industry, and that the best underwriting=20
years in the future decade may appear substandard against the=20
average year of the past decade.

     We have no magic formula to insulate our controlled=20
insurance companies against this deteriorating future.  Our=20
managers, particularly Phil Liesche, Bill Lyons, Roland Miller,=20
Floyd Taylor and Milt Thornton, have done a magnificent job of=20
swimming against the tide.  We have sacrificed much volume, but=20
have maintained a substantial underwriting superiority in=20
relation to industry-wide results.  The outlook at Berkshire is=20
for continued low volume.  Our financial position offers us=20
maximum flexibility, a very rare condition in the property-
casualty insurance industry.  And, at some point, should fear=20
ever prevail throughout the industry, our financial strength=20
could become an operational asset of immense value.

     We believe that GEICO Corporation, our major non-controlled=20
business operating in this field, is, by virtue of its extreme=20
and improving operating efficiency, in a considerably more=20
protected position than almost any other major insurer.  GEICO is=20
a brilliantly run implementation of a very important business=20
idea.


<i>Shareholder Designated Contributions</i>

     Our new program enabling shareholders to designate the=20
recipients of corporate charitable contributions was greeted with=20
extraordinary enthusiasm.  A copy of the letter sent October 14,=20
1981 describing this program appears on pages 51-53.  Of 932,206=20
shares eligible for participation (shares where the name of the=20
actual owner appeared on our stockholder record), 95.6%=20
responded.  Even excluding Buffet-related shares, the response=20
topped 90%.

     In addition, more than 3% of our shareholders voluntarily=20
wrote letters or notes, all but one approving of the program. =20
Both the level of participation and of commentary surpass any=20
shareholder response we have witnessed, even when such response=20
has been intensively solicited by corporate staff and highly paid=20
professional proxy organizations.  In contrast, your=20
extraordinary level of response occurred without even the nudge=20
of a company-provided return envelope.  This self-propelled=20
behavior speaks well for the program, and speaks well for our=20
shareholders.

     Apparently the owners of our corporation like both=20
possessing and exercising the ability to determine where gifts of=20
their funds shall be made.  The =93father-knows-best=94 school of=20
corporate governance will be surprised to find that none of our=20
shareholders sent in a designation sheet with instructions that=20
the officers of Berkshire - in their superior wisdom, of course -=20
make the decision on charitable funds applicable to his shares. =20
Nor did anyone suggest that his share of our charitable funds be=20
used to match contributions made by our corporate directors to=20
charities of the directors=92 choice (a popular, proliferating and=20
non-publicized policy at many large corporations).

     All told, $1,783,655 of shareholder-designed contributions=20
were distributed to about 675 charities.  In addition, Berkshire=20
and subsidiaries continue to make certain contributions pursuant=20
to local level decisions made by our operating managers.

     There will be some years, perhaps two or three out of ten,=20
when contributions by Berkshire will produce substandard tax=20
deductions - or none at all.  In those years we will not effect=20
our shareholder designated charitable program.  In all other=20
years we expect to inform you about October 10th of the amount=20
per share that you may designate.  A reply form will accompany=20
the notice, and you will be given about three weeks to respond=20
with your designation.  <i>To qualify, your shares must be=20
registered in your own name or the name of an owning trust,=20
corporation, partnership or estate, if applicable, on our=20
stockholder list of September 30th, or the Friday preceding if=20
such date falls on a Saturday or Sunday.</i>

     Our only disappointment with this program in 1981 was that=20
some of our shareholders, through no fault of their own, missed=20
the opportunity to participate.  The Treasury Department ruling=20
allowing us to proceed without tax uncertainty was received early=20
in October.  The ruling did not cover participation by=20
shareholders whose stock was registered in the name of nominees,=20
such as brokers, and additionally required that the owners of all=20
designating shares make certain assurances to Berkshire.  These=20
assurances could not be given us in effective form by nominee=20
holders.

     Under these circumstances, we attempted to communicate with=20
all of our owners promptly (via the October 14th letter) so that,=20
if they wished, they could prepare themselves to participate by=20
the November 13th record date.  It was particularly important=20
that this information be communicated promptly to stockholders=20
whose holdings were in nominee name, since they would not be=20
eligible unless they took action to re-register their shares=20
before the record date.

     Unfortunately, communication to such non-record shareholders=20
could take place only through the nominees.  We therefore=20
strongly urged those nominees, mostly brokerage houses, to=20
promptly transmit our letter to the real owners.  We explained=20
that their failure to do so could deprive such owners of an=20
important benefit.

     The results from our urgings would not strengthen the case=20
for private ownership of the U.S. Postal Service.  Many of our=20
shareholders never heard from their brokers (as some shareholders=20
told us after reading news accounts of the program).  Others were=20
forwarded our letter too late for action.

     One of the largest brokerage houses claiming to hold stock=20
for sixty of its clients (about 4% of our shareholder=20
population), apparently transmitted our letter about three weeks=20
after receipt - too late for any of the sixty to participate.=20
(Such lassitude did not pervade all departments of that firm; it=20
billed Berkshire for mailing services within six days of that=20
belated and ineffectual action.)

     We recite such horror stories for two reasons: (1) if you=20
wish to participate in future designated contribution programs,=20
be sure to have your stock registered in your name well before=20
September 30th; and (2) even if you don=92t care to participate and=20
prefer to leave your stock in nominee form, it would be wise to=20
have at least one share registered in your own name.  By so=20
doing, you can be sure that you will be notified of any important=20
corporate news at the same time as all other shareholders.

     The designated-contributions idea, along with many other=20
ideas that have turned out well for us, was conceived by Charlie=20
Munger, Vice Chairman of Berkshire and Chairman of Blue Chip. =20
Irrespective of titles, Charlie and I work as partners in=20
managing all controlled companies.  To almost a sinful degree, we=20
enjoy our work as managing partners.  And we enjoy having you as=20
our financial partners.


                                          Warren E. Buffett
